  "scientificChemicalCheck": {
    "controlledReferenceTrend": "For comparable standard acyl-substitution examples, acid chlorides are more reactive than anhydrides, which are more reactive than alkyl esters; carbonyl electrophilicity and relative stabilization/basicity of departing groups supply the explanation.",
    "limitation": "This is not a universal numerical rate law, nor an inference that rate and equilibrium are interchangeable. Different substituents, steric environments, protonation, solvent, nucleophile and catalytic conditions can defeat an uncontrolled comparison.",
